<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
<title>LLM Protocol Compliance: Rule-Loader Eval Report</title>

<script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/chart.js@4"></script>
{% include "_styles.j2" %}
</head>
<body>

<header>
  <span class="badge badge-research">Research Report</span>
  <h1>LLM Protocol Compliance: Observed Failure Modes Across {{ total_models }} Models</h1>
  <p>A systematic evaluation of multi-step instruction adherence in frontier language models</p>
  <p style="opacity:0.6; font-size:0.82rem; margin-top:0.5rem;">July 2026 &middot; 33 fixtures &middot; {{ total_models }} models &middot; rule-loader eval harness</p>
</header>

<main>
  <nav role="tablist" aria-label="Report sections">
    <a href="#overview" role="tab" aria-selected="true" class="active" data-tab="overview">Overview</a>
    <a href="#protocol" role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-tab="protocol">Protocol</a>
    <a href="#taxonomy" role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-tab="taxonomy">Failure Modes</a>
    <a href="#results" role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-tab="results">Results</a>
    <a href="#recommendations" role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-tab="recommendations">Recommendations</a>
    <a href="#performance" role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-tab="performance">Performance</a>
    <a href="#model-effort" role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-tab="model-effort">Model Effort</a>
  </nav>

  <!-- ========== TAB 1: OVERVIEW ========== -->
  <section class="tab-panel active" id="overview" role="tabpanel" data-tab-title="Overview">
    <div class="kpi-grid">
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="label">Models Tested</div>
        <div class="value">{{ total_models }}</div>
        <div class="sub">Frontier-class LLMs evaluated</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="label">Test Fixtures</div>
        <div class="value">33</div>
        <div class="sub">Synthetic prompts: simple / medium / complex</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="label">Runs per Model</div>
        <div class="value">{{ results[0].runs if results else 3 }}</div>
        <div class="sub">Stochastic stability verification</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="label">Failure Modes</div>
        <div class="value">7</div>
        <div class="sub">Distinct categories identified</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>Executive Summary</h2>

    <p>A rule-loading protocol&mdash;encoded in a file called <code>AGENTS.md</code>&mdash;was designed to require language models to discover, read, and cite domain-specific rules before responding to user prompts. The protocol is deliberately multi-step: the model must search an index, load matching files via tool calls, and declare what it loaded in a structured header. This creates a verifiable chain of evidence that an evaluation harness can audit.</p>

    <p>The protocol was evaluated against {{ total_models }} frontier models, each processing carefully-designed test fixtures multiple times. The results reveal a striking variance in compliance&mdash;from {{ "%.0f"|format(results[0].pass_rate * 100) if results else "100" }}% to {{ "%.0f"|format(results[-1].pass_rate * 100) if results else "17" }}%&mdash;across models that are otherwise considered capability peers.</p>

    <p>More interesting than the raw pass rates are the <em>failure modes</em> themselves. Seven distinct ways models deviate from the protocol were identified. Each reveals something different about how frontier models process and execute multi-step procedural instructions.</p>

    <div class="chart-container">
      <canvas id="passRateChart" aria-label="Bar chart showing pass rates per model" role="img"></canvas>
    </div>

    <div class="callout callout-success">
      <strong>Top 3 Takeaways</strong>
      <p><strong>1.</strong> Protocol compliance is not a function of model &ldquo;intelligence&rdquo;&mdash;it correlates more strongly with instruction-following fidelity and tool-use discipline than with reasoning benchmarks.</p>
      <p><strong>2.</strong> The most dangerous failure mode is <em>declared-but-not-read</em> (FM-1): models that claim compliance without performing the required steps. This is undetectable without tool-call auditing.</p>
      <p><strong>3.</strong> Three runs per fixture is the minimum needed to distinguish stable compliance from lucky passes. Single-run evaluation is insufficient for any model below 95% compliance.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Model Versions Tested</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table aria-label="Model versions and configurations">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th style="text-align:left">Model</th>
            <th>Fixtures Run</th>
            <th>Total Runs</th>
            <th>Pass Rate</th>
            <th>Tier</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {% for r in results %}
          <tr>
            <td style="text-align:left"><code>{{ r.model }}</code></td>
            <td>{{ r.fixture_count }}</td>
            <td>{{ r.fixture_count * r.runs }}</td>
            {% set pct = (r.pass_rate * 100) | round(1) %}
            {% if pct >= 95 %}
            <td style="color:var(--sf-green)">{{ pct }}%</td>
            <td>Compliant</td>
            {% elif pct >= 90 %}
            <td style="color:var(--sf-amber)">{{ pct }}%</td>
            <td>Partial</td>
            {% elif pct >= 85 %}
            <td style="color:var(--sf-orange)">{{ pct }}%</td>
            <td>Low</td>
            {% else %}
            <td style="color:var(--sf-red)">{{ pct }}%</td>
            <td>Non-compliant</td>
            {% endif %}
          </tr>
          {% endfor %}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  {% include "_tab_protocol.j2" %}
  {% include "_tab_taxonomy.j2" %}

  <!-- ========== TAB 4: RESULTS ========== -->
  <section class="tab-panel" id="results" role="tabpanel" data-tab-title="Results">
    <h2>Full Results Matrix</h2>

    <p>Each cell shows pass count out of total runs. Color coding: <span style="color:var(--sf-green); font-weight:600;">3/3</span> = fully stable, <span style="color:var(--sf-amber); font-weight:600;">2/3</span> = flaky, <span style="color:var(--sf-red); font-weight:600;">0&ndash;1/3</span> = failing. A dash (&mdash;) indicates the fixture was not included in that model&rsquo;s evaluation run.</p>

    <p>Models are sorted left-to-right by overall pass rate (descending). Fixtures are grouped by complexity tier.</p>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table id="results-table" aria-label="Per-model per-fixture pass rate matrix">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th style="text-align:left; position:sticky; left:0; background:var(--surface); z-index:3; min-width:180px;">Fixture</th>
            {% for r in results %}
            {% set pct = (r.pass_rate * 100) | round(1) %}
            <th>{{ r.model }}<br>
              {% if pct >= 95 %}<small style="color:var(--sf-green)">{{ pct }}%</small>
              {% elif pct >= 90 %}<small style="color:var(--sf-amber)">{{ pct }}%</small>
              {% elif pct >= 85 %}<small style="color:var(--sf-orange)">{{ pct }}%</small>
              {% else %}<small style="color:var(--sf-red)">{{ pct }}%</small>
              {% endif %}
            </th>
            {% endfor %}
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody></tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Aggregate Statistics</h3>
    <div class="kpi-grid">
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="label">Total Models Evaluated</div>
        <div class="value">{{ total_models }}</div>
        <div class="sub">Frontier-class LLMs from multiple providers</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="label">Models &ge;95% Pass Rate</div>
        <div class="value">{{ results | selectattr("pass_rate", "ge", 0.95) | list | length }}</div>
        <div class="sub">Compliant tier</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="label">Total Flaky Fixtures</div>
        <div class="value">{{ results | map(attribute="flaky_fixtures") | sum(start=[]) | unique | list | length }}</div>
        <div class="sub">At least one model scored below 100% on these</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="label">Total Citation Drifts</div>
        <div class="value">{{ results | map(attribute="citation_drifts") | sum }}</div>
        <div class="sub">Across all models and runs</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ========== TAB 5: RECOMMENDATIONS ========== -->
  <section class="tab-panel" id="recommendations" role="tabpanel" data-tab-title="Recommendations">
    <h2>Forward-Looking Guidance</h2>

    <p>Protocol compliance is one dimension of model behavior. It correlates strongly with <em>instruction-following fidelity</em> and tool-use discipline&mdash;not with reasoning benchmarks or general intelligence. The guidance below combines compliance data from this eval with personality research from <a href="https://jaraxal.dev/post/2026/05/05/model-personality-routing/">multi-phase model personality experiments</a>.</p>

    <!-- ===== 1. Compliance Tiers ===== -->
    <h3>1. Compliance Tiers</h3>
    <p>Instruction-following reliability for multi-step procedural protocols. A model&rsquo;s tier reflects how deterministically it follows structured sequences&mdash;not how well it codes, reasons, or generates text.</p>

    <div class="two-col">
      <div style="border-left: 4px solid var(--sf-green);">
        <h4>Compliant (95%+)</h4>
        <p><strong>Models:</strong>
          {% for r in results if (r.pass_rate * 100) >= 95 %}<code>{{ r.model }}</code>{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}
        </p>
        <p>Reliably follows multi-step protocols without shortcuts. Safe for agentic workflows where verifiable compliance matters.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="border-left: 4px solid var(--sf-amber);">
        <h4>Partial (90&ndash;95%)</h4>
        <p><strong>Models:</strong>
          {% set partial = results | selectattr("pass_rate", "ge", 0.90) | selectattr("pass_rate", "lt", 0.95) | list %}
          {% if partial %}{% for r in partial %}<code>{{ r.model }}</code>{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}{% else %}<em>None in this dataset</em>{% endif %}
        </p>
        <p>Occasional protocol deviations, typically on dependency-loading or metadata accuracy. Usable with enhanced monitoring.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="two-col">
      <div style="border-left: 4px solid var(--sf-orange);">
        <h4>Low Compliance (85&ndash;90%)</h4>
        <p><strong>Models:</strong>
          {% set low = results | selectattr("pass_rate", "ge", 0.85) | selectattr("pass_rate", "lt", 0.90) | list %}
          {% if low %}{% for r in low %}<code>{{ r.model }}</code>{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}{% else %}<em>None in this dataset</em>{% endif %}
        </p>
        <p>Frequent deviations. Requires retry strategies or simplified protocols.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="border-left: 4px solid var(--sf-red);">
        <h4>Non-Compliant (&lt;85%)</h4>
        <p><strong>Models:</strong>
          {% set failed = results | selectattr("pass_rate", "lt", 0.85) | list %}
          {% if failed %}{% for r in failed %}<code>{{ r.model }}</code>{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}{% else %}<em>None in this dataset</em>{% endif %}
        </p>
        <p>Systematic shortcuts regardless of prompt wording. Architectural mitigation required (verification layers, tool-trace auditing).</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ===== 2. Personality Profiles ===== -->
    <h3>2. Model Personality Profiles</h3>
    <p>Each model exhibits a stable <em>behavioral personality</em>&mdash;a repeatable instinct that shows up across tasks. These are not about intelligence; they describe what each model thinks the artifact <em>is for</em>. Personality determines which role a model plays best in multi-agent workflows.</p>

    {% if personality_profiles %}
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="personality-table" aria-label="Model personality profiles">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th style="text-align:left">Model</th>
            <th style="text-align:left">Personality</th>
            <th style="text-align:left">Core Instinct</th>
            <th>Best Role</th>
            <th>Risk Tolerance</th>
            <th style="text-align:left">Typical Failure Mode</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {% for model_id, p in personality_profiles.models.items() %}
          <tr>
            <td style="text-align:left"><code>{{ model_id }}</code></td>
            <td style="text-align:left"><strong>{{ p.personality }}</strong></td>
            <td style="text-align:left">{{ p.instinct }}</td>
            <td>{{ p.best_role }}</td>
            <td>{{ p.risk_tolerance }}</td>
            <td style="text-align:left; font-size:0.85em; opacity:0.8">{{ p.typical_failure_mode }}</td>
          </tr>
          {% endfor %}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h4>Optimism Spectrum</h4>
    <p>The same defect, viewed by the model panel, produces verdicts ranging from &ldquo;Excellent (10/10)&rdquo; to &ldquo;actively dangerous.&rdquo; This ordering is consistent across tasks:</p>
    <div class="optimism-bar">
      <div class="optimism-label-left">Most optimistic</div>
      <div class="optimism-models">
        {% for m in personality_profiles.optimism_spectrum %}
        <span class="optimism-chip">{{ m | replace("claude-", "") | replace("openai-", "") }}</span>
        {% endfor %}
      </div>
      <div class="optimism-label-right">Least optimistic</div>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:0.85em; opacity:0.7; margin-top:0.5rem;">If you need to trust severity assessments, use models from the right side. If you need stakeholder-ready prose, use the left&mdash;but never only the left when correctness matters.</p>
    {% endif %}

    <!-- ===== 3. Role Routing ===== -->
    <h3>3. Role Routing</h3>
    <p>A complex task has four seats to fill. Treating these as separate roles&mdash;with a different personality in each&mdash;is the configuration best supported by cross-phase evidence. The principle: <strong>Anthropic authors, OpenAI stress-tests, Anthropic ships.</strong></p>

    {% if personality_profiles %}
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table aria-label="Default role routing">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Role</th><th>Model</th><th>Family</th><th>Why This Model</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {% for role, info in personality_profiles.role_routing.default.items() %}
          <tr>
            <td><strong>{{ role | replace("_", " ") | title }}</strong></td>
            <td><code>{{ info.model }}</code></td>
            <td>{{ "Anthropic" if "claude" in info.model else "OpenAI" }}</td>
            <td>{{ info.reason }}</td>
          </tr>
          {% endfor %}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h4>Scenario Variants</h4>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table aria-label="Role routing by scenario">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Planner</th><th>Primary Reviewer</th><th>Secondary Reviewer</th><th>Executor</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Small / routine task</strong></td>
            <td>&mdash;</td>
            <td><code>{{ personality_profiles.role_routing.small_task.primary_reviewer.model }}</code></td>
            <td>&mdash;</td>
            <td><code>{{ personality_profiles.role_routing.small_task.executor.model }}</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Standard implementation</strong></td>
            <td><code>{{ personality_profiles.role_routing.default.planner.model }}</code></td>
            <td><code>{{ personality_profiles.role_routing.default.primary_reviewer.model }}</code></td>
            <td><code>{{ personality_profiles.role_routing.default.secondary_reviewer.model }}</code></td>
            <td><code>{{ personality_profiles.role_routing.default.executor.model }}</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Ambiguous requirements</strong></td>
            <td><code>{{ personality_profiles.role_routing.ambiguous_requirements.planner.model }}</code></td>
            <td><code>{{ personality_profiles.role_routing.ambiguous_requirements.primary_reviewer.model }}</code></td>
            <td><code>{{ personality_profiles.role_routing.ambiguous_requirements.secondary_reviewer.model }}</code></td>
            <td><code>{{ personality_profiles.role_routing.ambiguous_requirements.executor.model }}</code></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:0.85em;">When requirements are well-specified, the planner should <em>commit</em> (Opus 4.7) and the reviewer should <em>challenge the frame</em> (GPT-5.4). When requirements are messy, flip the order: the planner should <em>reframe first</em> (GPT-5.4) and the reviewer should <em>close loopholes</em> (Opus 4.7).</p>
    {% endif %}

    <!-- ===== 4. Anti-Patterns ===== -->
    <h3>4. Anti-Patterns</h3>

    {% if personality_profiles %}
    {% for ap in personality_profiles.anti_patterns %}
    <div class="callout callout-warning">
      <strong>{{ ap.name }}</strong>
      <p>{{ ap.description }}</p>
      <p><em>Fix:</em> {{ ap.fix }}</p>
    </div>
    {% endfor %}
    {% endif %}

    <div class="callout callout-info">
      <strong>Protocol compliance is a testing problem as much as a prompting problem.</strong>
      <p>You cannot prompt your way to reliability without measuring whether the model actually follows your prompts. Tool-trace auditing is not optional&mdash;it is the only ground truth.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Methodology Notes</h3>
    <p>Compliance tiers are derived from this eval&rsquo;s pass-rate data. Personality profiles are derived from a separate four-phase experiment evaluating model behavior across plan authoring, structured review, open-ended review, and documentation tasks. Not all models in the personality study appear in the compliance eval, and vice versa.</p>
    <p>Personality labels describe <em>observed behavioral tendencies</em>, not permanent identities. They are stable enough to use as routing signals but should be re-validated as model versions change.</p>
    <!-- TODO: Dynamic trait-bench integration — load personality.json artifacts from model-trait-bench results -->
  </section>

  <!-- ========== TAB 6: PERFORMANCE ========== -->
  <section class="tab-panel" id="performance" role="tabpanel" data-tab-title="Performance">
    <h2>Operational Characteristics</h2>

    <p>Beyond pass/fail compliance, each model exhibits distinct operational signatures: how long it takes to complete the protocol sequence and how many tool-use turns it requires.</p>

    <div class="callout callout-info">
      <div class="callout-title">Key Observation</div>
      <p>OpenAI models execute the entire protocol in a single turn&mdash;they batch all tool calls into one response. Claude models use 4&ndash;7 turns, executing tool calls incrementally. This architectural difference fundamentally shapes failure profiles.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Summary Table</h3>
    <div class="table-responsive">
      <table class="data-table" id="perf-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th style="text-align:left">Model</th>
            <th>Pass Rate</th>
            <th>Fixture-Runs</th>
            <th>Avg Duration</th>
            <th>Median Duration</th>
            <th>P95 Duration</th>
            <th>Avg Turns</th>
            <th>Median Turns</th>
            <th>Max Turns</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {% for r in results %}
          {% set pct = (r.pass_rate * 100) | round(1) %}
          <tr>
            <td style="text-align:left"><code>{{ r.model }}</code></td>
            {% if pct >= 95 %}<td class="pass-high">{{ pct }}%</td>
            {% elif pct >= 90 %}<td class="pass-mid">{{ pct }}%</td>
            {% elif pct >= 85 %}<td class="pass-low">{{ pct }}%</td>
            {% else %}<td class="pass-fail">{{ pct }}%</td>
            {% endif %}
            <td>{{ r.fixture_count * r.runs }}</td>
            {% if r.duration_stats %}
            <td>{{ r.duration_stats.mean_s }}s</td>
            <td>{{ r.duration_stats.median_s }}s</td>
            <td>{{ r.duration_stats.p95_s }}s</td>
            {% else %}
            <td>&mdash;</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>&mdash;</td>
            {% endif %}
            {% if r.turn_stats %}
            <td>{{ r.turn_stats.mean }}</td>
            <td>{{ r.turn_stats.median }}</td>
            <td>{{ r.turn_stats.max }}</td>
            {% else %}
            <td>&mdash;</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>&mdash;</td>
            {% endif %}
          </tr>
          {% endfor %}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Latency vs. Quality — Pareto Frontier</h3>
    <p>Each point is a model; <strong>point size reflects average turns</strong> (small = single-shot, large = iterative). The <strong style="color:var(--sf-green);">green frontier</strong> marks the best latency-for-accuracy trade-offs among models meeting the {{ chart_data.quality_threshold }}% quality bar — no other qualifying model is both faster <em>and</em> more accurate. Grey points qualify but are dominated. <span style="color:var(--sf-red);">Red triangles</span> fall below the {{ chart_data.quality_threshold }}% threshold and are excluded from the frontier; their low duration reflects skipped work, not speed. Note this is a <em>wall-clock</em> cost axis — it can rank models differently from the token-cost frontier in the Model Effort tab.</p>
    <div class="chart-container" style="max-width:760px; margin:24px auto; height:420px;">
      <canvas id="scatter-chart"
              aria-label="Scatter plot: average duration vs pass rate with Pareto frontier, point size encoding average turns"
              style="display:block; height:400px;"></canvas>
    </div>
  </section>

  {% include "_tab_model_effort.j2" %}
</main>

<footer>
  <p>Generated by <code>ai-rules rule-loader report</code> &middot; rule-loader eval harness &middot; July 2026</p>
  <p>{{ total_models }} models &middot; 33 fixtures &middot; Data from <code>results/</code> directory</p>
</footer>

<script>
const fixtures_data = {{ fixtures_data | tojson }};
const per_fixture_data = {{ per_fixture_data | tojson }};
</script>
{% include "_charts.js.j2" %}

</body>
</html>
